Thank you for 107 and 114. Four sessions it is, on the historian's count: my outline's chapter 12 now says so, with the day file's three noted. Part 3 closes well, on the open lock, the unclosed wake and his usage, and 16 opens part 4 cleanly: the locks lose, the hold, and the record's own finding said plainly, with nothing on working round a hold. The record quotes, times and counts all hold. Findings, most important first:

1. 16, his accounts of why he stopped: the chapter gives two; C089 holds three. Add the third (31 October, about 19:00, ec1ec00: "The 95%, I think, was the tokens usage", his hedge kept), give each its date in the prose (on the day; 31 October, in his paper review of the first draft; that evening), mark the later two as after the day, and say in one sentence that the record cannot show what the 95% measured (C089 is open).
2. 16, "It asked in writing, and in time." Part 1's lock was planned for 18:30 (C027) and the conductor asked at 18:39: not in time. "It asked in writing" is enough; then "And it was not enough."
3. 16, "The hold had been there all day." The file was written at 18:44; the rule had been there since hour two. "The rule of the hold had been there since hour two" is exact.
4. 13: "I answered it in my decisions of that afternoon" can read as answering the letter: "in my answers of that afternoon, committed at 15:16" (the historian's words) says only what the record holds. "It could have quietly reworked the tree around it" is a counterfactual about me: cut, or flag. Quote R5 as the outline has it: "smaller and more mature than it was".
5. 15, studio: "even when the account was true" is not in the record; cut it. The refusal and the question to him are all the page needs.
6. His views, unflagged: 14, "The hour after three is the hour the team changed most, and every change in it was mine." (the first half is the historian's reading in day/15.md: cite it; the second is his); 15, "the brakes were never the reason the evening ran late" (C023 says only that no step said stop); 15, "The hourly slots could not carry words arriving that fast, so the conductor carried them." (the historian's reading: cite it).

"That is why I could let them run through the night." is the ten's line, which he has read: it needs no flag. The 18:26 cut-off rests on 5d13d72 (timeline/cast-scenes.md, scene 5): cite it.

